Q: what is the best way to read single line files in Java A: <h2>NIO.2</h2>
<p>Modern Java now offers more convenient file-handling with the NIO.2 features. See <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/technotes/guides/io/index.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Java documentation</a> and other <a href="https://www.ecosia.org/search?q=java%20nio.2" rel="nofollow noreferrer">sources of info</a>.</p>
<p>NIO.2 makes reading all the lines of a file trivial. So, I would read all possible lines, then check that you did indeed get only a single line. Regarding inputs to your app that lay outside your control: <strong>Trust, but verify</strong>.</p>
<p>You may encounter even small issues such as someone editing the one-line text while inadvertently appending a blank line. So eliminate blank lines before reporting an error.</p>
<p>Call <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Paths.html#get(java.lang.String,java.lang.String...)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Paths.get</code></a> to identify the file. Call <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Files.html#readAllLines(java.nio.file.Path,java.nio.charset.Charset)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Files.readAllLines</code></a> to read the file, parsing each line as a separate <code>String</code> object.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Path filePath = Paths.get( &quot;/Users/basil_dot_work/example.txt&quot; );
try
{
    final List &lt; String &gt; lines = 
        Files
            .readAllLines( filePath , StandardCharsets.UTF_8 )
            .stream()
            .filter( line -&gt; ! line.isBlank() )
            .toList();
    if ( lines.size() != 1 ) { … handle error … }
    String input = lines.getFirst() ;  // In Java 21+, per Sequenced Collections. 
    … // Further processing.
}
catch ( IOException e )
{
    System.err.println( &quot;Error reading file: &quot; + e.getMessage() );
}
</code></pre>

Q: My code keeps returning an error that says &quot;e is undefined&quot; whenever I run it? A: <p>In the line <code>javasctipt var code = e.keyCode || e.which;</code> the variable <code>e</code> is undefined.</p>
<p>You have to define an event handler:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const element = document.querySelector(the_selector_to_get_an_HTML_element);
element.addEventListener(&quot;keydown&quot;, event =&gt; (
   const code = event.code;
   // do something depending on the code values
   // you don't need wich and should not use it as it is deprecated
));
</code></pre>
<p>See also: <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/KeyboardEvent" rel="nofollow noreferrer">KeyboardEvent</a>.</p>
<p>Note that you cannot even find <code>which</code> in the documentation. But you still can find the information on the <a href="https://www.w3schools.com/jsref/event_key_which.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">deprecation</a>.</p>
<p>There are many other problems in your code. In particular, you need <code>const</code>, not <code>var</code>.</p>

Q: How to stop member function returning T&amp; being defined for T = void (and is supposed to be) A: <p>Another option is use <code>auto&amp;</code> as the return type. This works because you defer deducing the return type to the body instantiation, which happens only when the concept is satisfied. The advantage over the <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79916873/2805305">solutions by HolyBlackCat</a> is that you get something less verbose, the disadvantage is that you lose being explicit in the return type.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>auto&amp; operator[](std::integral auto i) const noexcept requires (!std::is_void_v&lt;T&gt;)
{
    return m_data[i];
}    
</code></pre>
